      <section class="band band--w">
        <div class="wrap">
          <article class="art">
            <p class="lede">
              Time-to-hire tells you how fast a process moved. It does not tell you whether the process was any good.
              This is about the number that does. And the thing we had backwards about it for two years.
            </p>

            <p>
              Every talent team reports time-to-hire. Most report cost-per-hire and offer-acceptance alongside it. They
              are real numbers and they measure real things, and none of them will tell you whether the search you just
              ran was sound.
            </p>

            <p>Consider two roles that both closed in forty days.</p>

            <p>
              The first ran four interviews. One candidate, three rounds, an offer accepted. The hiring manager moved
              quickly because the shortlist was right and she could see why.
            </p>

            <p>
              The second ran twelve. Nine candidates, most of whom were never going to clear round two, and a manager
              who by week five had stopped trusting anything the pipeline sent her. She hired someone in the end. She
              also stopped opening the emails.
            </p>

            <figure class="fig">
              <svg
                class="fig__svg"
                viewBox="0 0 330 80"
                fill="none"
