            <h2>The quietest number on the dashboard</h2>

            <p>
              Interview-to-hire ratio counts how many people your team had to meet before one of them was right. It is
              usually buried three tabs deep in an ATS report, and it is the most honest figure in there, because it is
              the only one that cannot be improved by working harder.
            </p>

            <p>
              You can cut time-to-hire by chasing people. You can cut cost-per-hire by using cheaper channels. The ratio
              does not move for either. It only moves when the shortlist gets better, which means it is not really a
              measure of your pipeline at all. It is a measure of how well the role was understood before anyone started
              looking.
            </p>

            <blockquote class="pull">
              Time-to-hire measures your speed.
              <em>Interview-to-hire measures your aim.</em>
            </blockquote>

            <h2>What we had backwards</h2>

            <p>
              For the first two years we read a poor ratio as a candidate-quality problem. If a team was meeting nine
              people to hire one, the obvious reading was that six of those nine should never have been on the list, and
              the fix was better screening.
            </p>

            <p>That was wrong, or at least it was the smaller half of the answer.</p>

            <p>
              When we went back through the mandates where the ratio was worst, the pattern was not weak candidates. In
              most of them the candidates were fine. What those searches had in common was that nobody had written down
              what good looked like before the search started. So the bar was assembled in the room, one interview at a
              time, and it moved.
            </p>

            <p>
              A manager who has not written the bar down does not have a fixed one. She has a comparison. Candidate four
              is judged against candidates one to three rather than against the job, and every new person shifts the
              frame slightly. That is why those searches feel like they are converging on something and never arrive.
            </p>

            <figure class="fig">
              <svg
                class="fig__svg"
                viewBox="0 0 300 106"
                fill="none"
                xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"
                aria-hidden="true"
                ><rect x="0" y="0" width="288.0" height="10" rx="5" fill="#DA0007" opacity="1"></rect><rect
                  x="0"
                  y="26"
                  width="222.0"
                  height="10"
                  rx="5"
                  fill="#8E8A80"
                  opacity="1"></rect><rect x="0" y="52" width="183.0" height="10" rx="5" fill="#8E8A80" opacity="1"
                ></rect><rect x="0" y="78" width="69.0" height="10" rx="5" fill="#C6C2B8" opacity="1"></rect></svg
              >
              <figcaption>
                Across the mandates where the ratio was worst, <b
                  >the strongest correlation was whether the bar existed in writing before sourcing began</b
                >. Not candidate quality, which barely moved the number at all. Relative strength of association, our
                own mandates.
              </figcaption>
            </figure>

            <h2>Where three comes from</h2>

            <p>
              Across the searches we run, three interviews per hire is roughly where a calibrated process settles. Not
              because three is a magic number, but because that is what happens when the bar is fixed in advance and
              everyone in the loop is reading the same one.
            </p>

            <p>
              It is worth saying plainly that this is not a target, and a team that games it will get nothing. Sending
              fewer people does not improve alignment; it just moves the failure later, into a hire nobody was sure
              about. The ratio is a symptom. Treating a symptom is how you end up with a number that looks healthy
              attached to a team that is guessing.
            </p>

            <h2>What to do with it on Monday</h2>

            <p>Three things, in the order of how little they cost.</p>

            <p>
              <b>Put the ratio next to time-to-hire in the same report.</b> Not instead of it — next to it. The two numbers
              together tell you something neither says alone, and it takes an afternoon to add the column.
            </p>

            <p>
              <b>Write the bar down before you source.</b> Four or five dimensions, weighted, agreed with the hiring manager
              in the intake conversation. It does not need to be elaborate. It needs to exist before the first CV, because
              a bar written after the first CV is a description of that CV.
            </p>

            <p>
              <b>Keep the reasoning, not just the outcome.</b> Most teams record who was rejected. Almost none record why,
              in a form anyone can read six months later. That record is what stops the eleventh search starting from the
              same blank page as the first.
            </p>

            <p>
              None of this makes hiring fast. It makes it legible, which is the thing that eventually makes it fast.
            </p>

            <p class="signoff">Belong. Build. Become.</p>
          </article>

